<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gallipoli</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gelibolu, Gallipoli peninsula, Thracian Chersonese</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gallipoli (Turkish Gelibolu) is a town and peninsula on the European shore of the Dardanelles in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, known in antiquity as the Thracian Chersonese. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> seized the town in 1354 after an earthquake damaged its walls, gaining their first lasting foothold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In 1915–1916 the peninsula was the scene of the Allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli-campaign">Gallipoli campaign</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gallipoli.jpg" alt="Gallipoli" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gallipoli_peninsula_from_space.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
